window.docContents[2].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow":{"contents":"\n\nRemoving viscosity from the momentum law leaves the incompressible Euler\nequations:\n\n\n\nThe equation describes an ideal fluid with no viscous dissipation. Its\nnonlinearity is the vector analogue of Burgers self-transport, while pressure\ncouples all components through incompressibility.\n\nThe Euler momentum residual is the vector fieldThe pointwise momentum balance is the equation .\n\nThe residual vanishes exactly when\n\nMove the force term across the equality. This is an identity in the additive\ngroup of velocity vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Euler, Symmetry, and Boussinesq Flow","header":"8.4.1. Incompressible Euler Flow","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Classical-and-Weak-Heat-Flow":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems","header":"6.8.1. Classical and Weak Heat Flow","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Classical-and-Weak-Heat-Flow"}});